And as we continue to grow
Google Cloud Platform,

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:57.920
we wanted to provide the
customer with a resource

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:00.440
that they can have access
to when it comes to building

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:02.060
their application on GCP.

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:03.950
So in short words,
our ultimate goal

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:06.170
is to help the customer be
successful on Google Cloud

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:09.050
Platform and own that end-to-end
technical relationship

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:10.070
with them.

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:12.620
We're a single stateful
interface to the customer.

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:14.620
And because we're that
stateful interface,

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:16.880
it allows us to provide
more strategic guidance

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:19.430
to help them make these
technical decisions regarding

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:20.460
Google Cloud Platform.

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:23.120
MARK MANDEL: So I'm guessing, in
theory then, if I'm a customer

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:24.661
and I'm thinking to
myself, I'm going

00:09:24.661 --> 00:09:27.672
to build the next big thing,
whatever that happens to be.

00:09:27.672 --> 00:09:29.380
And I'm thinking, how
do I store my data?

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:30.830
Or how do I scale this out?

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:32.222
Or how do I do authentication?

00:09:32.222 --> 00:09:34.430
I can come to you with my
ideas, and you can be like,

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:36.800
this is good, or this is
bad, or something in between.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:37.550
JONATHAN CHAM: That's perfect.

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:38.660
Yeah, that's exactly what it is.

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:40.100
Actually, would you
like an analogy?

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:41.183
I can give a good analogy.

00:09:41.183 --> 00:09:41.964
MARK MANDEL: Sure.

00:09:41.964 --> 00:09:44.130
JONATHAN CHAM: All right,
so it's good, not perfect,

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:47.850
but I consider us like doctors
and our customers are patients.

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:50.540
So let me ask you
real quick, what

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:51.860
do you look for in a doctor?

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:52.901
MARK MANDEL: Lying to me.

00:09:52.901 --> 00:09:54.640
I like my doctors to lie to me.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:57.050
JONATHAN CHAM: You want
them to be untrustworthy.

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:58.400
MARK MANDEL: I just want them
to tell me that everything's

00:09:58.400 --> 00:09:59.650
going to be fine.

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:01.130
JONATHAN CHAM: OK, OK, yeah.

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:02.005
There's some of that.

00:10:02.005 --> 00:10:04.505
We like to tell our patients
everything is going to be fine.

00:10:04.505 --> 00:10:06.040
But when you look
for a doctor you

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:08.540
want someone who you can trust,
someone who's knowledgeable,

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:10.490
someone that knows
you very well, right?

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:12.260
So listen and ask
good questions.

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:16.700
And so imagine you come in
and you say, hey, Dr. Jonathan

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:20.810
Cham, I have MCS syndrome, which
stands for Massive Container

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:21.740
Syndrome.

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:23.130
Have you ever heard of that?

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:24.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I mean,
is that Kubernetes?

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:25.978
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
what it is that?

00:10:25.978 --> 00:10:27.462
I don't know what that is.

00:10:27.462 --> 00:10:29.420
JONATHAN CHAM: Massive
Container Syndrome, it's

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:31.840
a syndrome where you have
just way too many containers.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:33.920
So what are your symptoms?

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:35.990
Spending too much time
managing containers,

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:39.050
can't scale up and down
my app quickly enough,

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:41.690
my spreadsheet that lists
where my containers are running

00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:43.340
is getting too complex.

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:47.034
These are symptoms of MCS,
and it's getting widespread.

00:10:47.034 --> 00:10:47.700
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:49.235
And so they come
to you with this.

00:10:49.235 --> 00:10:51.110
And you're like, I have
the solution for you.

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:51.540
JONATHAN CHAM: Yes.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:53.540
And, of course, I'll ask
a couple more questions

00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:55.027
about their goal.

00:10:55.027 --> 00:10:56.360
What other symptoms do you have?

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:58.220
And after listening,
we kind of do

00:10:58.220 --> 00:11:00.320
some diagnosis and prescribing.

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:01.810
Maybe here's some medication.

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:03.110
It's called Kubernetes.

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:03.860
It's effective.

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:04.480
It's potent.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:06.680
It will help you
with that syndrome.

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:08.550
The only side effect
is that you'll

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:10.550
have to manage all that
infrastructure yourself,

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:12.950
install, configure, update,
and build out the Kubernetes

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:17.120
cluster, very affordable,
accepts all health insurances.

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:21.570
Another medication, a drug
called Google Container Engine,

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:24.770
no side effects, higher
efficacy, but just as potent

00:11:24.770 --> 00:11:25.820
as Kubernetes.

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:27.770
It will work much
faster, but the drug

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:29.640
costs just marginally
more than Kubernetes.

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:32.000
So you get the analogy.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:34.520
The sense is-- we come in,
listen to their problems,

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:38.540
and try to provide
some diagnosis on what

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:39.290
they want to do.

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:41.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes, so
we interviewed someone

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:45.320
that was a strategic customer
engineer working specifically

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:47.480
for platinum customers.

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:49.550
And I'm curious about--

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:51.890
to become a platinum
customer, you actually

00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:56.420
need to use the Cloud to a
point where if you overuse it,

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:57.650
we notice.

00:11:57.650 --> 00:11:59.950
What about customer engineers?

00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:01.180
Is this for everyone?

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:03.440
Is this for like,
can a new customer

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:05.292
just come directly and get one?

00:12:05.292 --> 00:12:07.250
JONATHAN CHAM: Yeah, so
a lot of new customers,

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:09.380
typically as we're
growing, we are

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:12.800
starting to assign customer
engineers to a lot of accounts.

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:15.710
And what that means is that
they might hear from us.

00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:18.500
If they want to be proactive,
I know this sounds a little

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:22.310
sales-y, but you go to the
cloud.google.com website,

00:12:22.310 --> 00:12:27.410
go to /sales, and you tell them
who you are and what you do,

00:12:27.410 --> 00:12:29.894
there's a very, very good
chance that you already have

00:12:29.894 --> 00:12:31.810
a customer engineer that
you don't know about.

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:32.476
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:12:32.476 --> 00:12:34.970
So basically anyone who is
thinking about getting involved

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:38.990
with customer engineers or wants
to get started on the program,

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:41.670
they're able to do that now
and go there and get started.

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:42.503
JONATHAN CHAM: Yeah.

00:12:42.503 --> 00:12:45.650
If you're part of a
legitimate organization that's

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:46.700
using Google Cloud--

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:49.700
if you're running your own
personal blog web site,

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:52.130
you're probably not going
to get a customer engineer.

00:12:52.130 --> 00:12:53.330
I don't know.

00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:54.830
Do you guys have a
customer engineer

00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:55.912
for your gcppodcast.com?

00:12:55.912 --> 00:12:57.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: No,
but maybe we should.

00:12:57.620 --> 00:12:58.828
MARK MANDEL: Maybe we should.

00:12:58.828 --> 00:12:59.926
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:12:59.926 --> 00:13:01.884
JONATHAN CHAM: Well, I'll
find out who that is.

00:13:01.884 --> 00:13:04.582
But yeah, typically the
size of the organization

00:13:04.582 --> 00:13:05.540
usually doesn't matter.

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:07.374
It's a matter of how
much are you using GCP.

00:13:07.374 --> 00:13:09.706
MARK MANDEL: So that's actually
an interesting question.

00:13:09.706 --> 00:13:12.170
You say this is for people
who are already using GCP.

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:14.360
What if somebody
isn't yet using GCP

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:16.640
but wants to sort of
have help onboarding.

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:17.840
Is that also in your role?

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:20.089
JONATHAN CHAM: Yes, that's
absolutely part of my role.

00:13:20.089 --> 00:13:22.940
And that's actually part
of our typical engagement.

00:13:22.940 --> 00:13:26.510
A lot of these initial meetings
will have customers who are not

00:13:26.510 --> 00:13:29.420
GCP customers reach out
through that same website,

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:31.280
cloud.google.com/sales.

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:34.820
And they'll say, hey, I'm
using AWS, or I'm using Azure.

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:36.590
I'm interested in
looking at GCP.

00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:39.320
And we'll go and
tell them about GCP,

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:41.960
but more importantly learn about
what they're interested in,

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:44.217
like, why do they
want to talk to us?

00:13:44.217 --> 00:13:46.550
A lot of them want to talk
about multi-cloud strategies.

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:49.010
They want to talk about
having high availability

00:13:49.010 --> 00:13:50.160
across vendors.

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:53.420
So not all clouds are
perfect, and a lot

00:13:53.420 --> 00:13:56.010
of times customers who might be
happy with their current Cloud

00:13:56.010 --> 00:14:00.290
vendor want to have a second
cloud vendor just as backup.

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:02.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
that makes me think.

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:06.170
What does it look like
when, if I'm a customer

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:09.320
and I want to engage
with a customer engineer,

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:14.227
how would the doctor visit with
like following your metaphor?

00:14:14.227 --> 00:14:15.560
MARK MANDEL: Do you wear a coat?

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:16.310
Do you wear a white coat?

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:18.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh,
yeah, that would be cool.

00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:19.676
JONATHAN CHAM:
Yeah, first of all,

00:14:19.676 --> 00:14:22.570
I make sure I bring my lab
coat with me that has my name,

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:24.955
Dr. Jonathan Cham.

00:14:24.955 --> 00:14:26.330
I alluded to some
of this earlier

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:27.590
about the doctor analogy.

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:29.697
A lot of customers
reach out to us.

00:14:29.697 --> 00:14:31.280
And sometimes we'll
reach out to them.

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:34.760
But initial meetings for
people who don't know GCP--

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:36.560
we just discuss about
what their goals are.

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:38.660
I mentioned about
multi-cloud strategies.

00:14:38.660 --> 00:14:40.610
But they also want
to hear from Google.

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:43.780
Google itself has a lot
of impact in the industry.

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:45.849
They want to hear about
trends within the market.

00:14:45.849 --> 00:14:48.140
Just recently I was at a
health care executive briefing

00:14:48.140 --> 00:14:50.480
where I spoke about the
need to collect information.

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:52.250
And there was a
Kaggle competition

00:14:52.250 --> 00:14:54.680
about predicting patient
health care costs.

00:14:54.680 --> 00:14:56.600
And they were super
excited about that.

00:14:56.600 --> 00:14:59.030
So they were really
surprised about the maturity

00:14:59.030 --> 00:15:00.800
of where machine
learning was these days.

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:03.090
So after an initial
meeting, usually there's

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:05.340
something like, oh,
OK, this sounds great.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:07.140
GCP is cool.

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:10.740
Let's see how much I will gain,
or how much I'll save money.

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:13.650
What type of value add benefits
will I get from moving?

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:15.600
So a lot of times
they'll make us

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:18.970
do a cost analysis with
all the other vendors.

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:20.700
So we do that a lot.

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:23.040
And once they've committed
to using Google Cloud

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:26.430
after they've done kind of the
analysis, their paper analysis,

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:28.680
they go, OK, we've
decided Google Cloud

00:15:28.680 --> 00:15:29.850
Platform is the way to go.

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:30.870
We like BigQuery.

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:33.720
It's awesome, totally
unique and differentiated.

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:34.950
We like data flow.

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:36.450
Tell me how to build my app.

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:38.970
So we'll sit in a room for
like three hours talking

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:42.390
about what they're doing
today, where they want to go,

00:15:42.390 --> 00:15:44.240
how much storage
do they have today.

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:45.340
Just a lot of questions.

00:15:45.340 --> 00:15:47.850
And a lot of iteration
goes on and on

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:49.200
throughout those meetings.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:52.740
And in terms of kind of how do
you take that into production,

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:55.890
I mean, what do you guys think
is the percentage of effort

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:59.580
between taking it and building
an app in Dev versus building

00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:00.627
an app in production?

00:16:00.627 --> 00:16:03.002
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I mean, do
you care if it actually runs?

00:16:03.002 --> 00:16:05.720
JONATHAN CHAM: Yes.

00:16:05.720 --> 00:16:07.830
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Then, I mean, knowing

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:10.290
how hard things
normally are, I guess

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:13.440
it depends a lot on whether
you're running on App Engine,

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:15.820
or Container Engine, Compute
Engine, and stuff like that.

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:18.030
But I'd say that,
yeah, it's probably

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:20.760
50% more time I'm making sure
that everything is actually

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:24.250
monitored, the alerts are
set up, and stuff like that.

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:26.640
JONATHAN CHAM: Yeah, a
lot of the times companies

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:29.340
have kind of like their
minimal viable product.

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:31.980
They do a little quick demo
running on a single instance,

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:34.232
and like, oh my gosh, this
is what we want to do.

00:16:34.232 --> 00:16:36.190
But when it comes to
building out, putting that

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:39.090
into production for thousands,
or hundreds of thousands

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:42.370
of customers, they need to focus
on things like reliability,

00:16:42.370 --> 00:16:44.820
availability, manageability,
performance, security,

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:45.820
scalability.

00:16:45.820 --> 00:16:47.760
That list goes on and on and on.

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:49.560
So a lot of those
discussions kind of

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:51.120
reiterate back and
forth, making sure

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:54.420
that as they deploy this
into production that they're

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:56.680
successful in doing so.

00:16:56.680 --> 00:16:58.789
MARK MANDEL: Do you
tend to also engage?

00:16:58.789 --> 00:16:59.580
Like, I'm guessing.

00:16:59.580 --> 00:17:01.560
Because there's multiple teams,
and we've had some on here,

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:03.720
like maybe CRE, that's
some big customers,

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:06.526
but like support teams, things
like that, you also engage

00:17:06.526 --> 00:17:07.650
with them across the board?

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:09.240
JONATHAN CHAM: Oh,
absolutely, yeah.

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:11.641
There's a lot of different
people that are involved.

00:17:11.641 --> 00:17:14.099
When I say all of this, it
makes it sound like I'm actually

00:17:14.099 --> 00:17:15.089
doing all the work.

00:17:15.089 --> 00:17:16.829
But a lot of what
we do is just make

00:17:16.829 --> 00:17:20.119
sure we own that end-to-end
technical relationship.

00:17:20.119 --> 00:17:23.417
So sometimes if the customer
has a specific, let's say,

00:17:23.417 --> 00:17:25.500
Kubernetes error, we're
going to bring in support.

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:26.875
That's not something
that I would

00:17:26.875 --> 00:17:28.470
be troubleshooting for them.

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:30.690
Sometimes for large
platinum customers,

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:33.360
like you had mentioned before,
they have a strategic customer

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:36.030
engineer who they've
actually paid for

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:39.960
to be their hands-on person that
will actually sometimes write

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:42.180
code, make sure
everything's up and running,

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:43.579
log all the feature requests.

00:17:43.579 --> 00:17:45.870
Because these platinum
customers have a lot of features

00:17:45.870 --> 00:17:47.860
that they want that
we might not have.

00:17:47.860 --> 00:17:50.390
So just making sure that they're
happy, they do some of that

00:17:50.390 --> 00:17:50.890
as well.

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:53.820
So for us, we're kind of
just owning that end-to-end.

00:17:53.820 --> 00:17:56.750
From beginning all the way
to their next big project,

00:17:56.750 --> 00:17:58.570
we want to be that
single point of contact.

00:17:58.570 --> 00:18:02.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So what is the
profile of a customer engineer?

00:18:02.190 --> 00:18:03.540
Are you engineers?

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:04.890
Are you salesmen?

00:18:04.890 --> 00:18:05.970
Are you both?

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:08.220
Are you actually doctors?

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:11.120
JONATHAN CHAM: Well, I think
it's a really good question.

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:14.460
I think they adjusted our
titles to be customer engineers,

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:16.260
because a lot of
us really aren't

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:18.470
coming from sales backgrounds.

00:18:18.470 --> 00:18:21.300
A lot of these people that we're
hiring are literally engineers.

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:23.940
Some of them were
engineers at Google.

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:25.500
I sit next to a
Google Developer.

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:28.350
And he decided, hey, I want
to be more customer-facing.

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:31.350
So the people who we hire--

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:33.480
by the way, we are
hiring a ton of people.

00:18:33.480 --> 00:18:37.260
So go to google.com, look
for customer engineer,

00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:39.311
and see if you can apply.

00:18:39.311 --> 00:18:41.310
But yeah, we're looking
for all types of people.

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:43.860
And you absolutely
do not need to have

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:45.140
kind of a sales background.

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:48.750
You just need to be able
to talk intelligently

00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:51.170
in front of customers
and not be awkward.

00:18:51.170 --> 00:18:52.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That kind of sounds

00:18:52.670 --> 00:18:54.660
not that different from
the role of developer

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:56.386
advocate, where we're
engineers, but we

00:18:56.386 --> 00:18:58.010
need to talk, not in
front of customers

00:18:58.010 --> 00:19:00.259
necessarily, but in
front of lots of people.

00:19:00.259 --> 00:19:01.300
MARK MANDEL: That's true.

00:19:01.300 --> 00:19:01.950
JONATHAN CHAM: Yeah.

00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:03.150
MARK MANDEL: We don't
have the responsibility

00:19:03.150 --> 00:19:05.160
of making sure production
systems stay up, though.

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:06.743
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We
definitely do not.

00:19:06.743 --> 00:19:08.400
JONATHAN CHAM: Yeah, yeah.

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:10.560
Like the doctor, we're
there for the good times

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:11.700
and the bad times.

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:14.170
When things break,
guess who they call?

00:19:14.170 --> 00:19:16.110
They'll say, hey
Jonathan, are things down?

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:17.340
You need to help us.

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:19.290
Support's not responding,
things like that.

00:19:19.290 --> 00:19:21.804
So it's my job to help, what
they call, herd the cats.

00:19:21.804 --> 00:19:24.220
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so that's
actually a really good point.

00:19:24.220 --> 00:19:25.800
So it's not just
a, hey, I'm going

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:27.090
to help this person on board.

00:19:27.090 --> 00:19:29.437
It's also the ongoing
relationship throughout time.

00:19:29.437 --> 00:19:30.770
JONATHAN CHAM: Absolutely, yeah.

00:19:30.770 --> 00:19:33.060
It's a very strategic
role, meaning

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:36.540
that you're with them throughout
many different initiatives, not

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:37.560
just one.

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:40.080
And I think that's kind
of where-- maybe where

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:43.300
DevRel or developer advocate
might differ from us is, yeah,

00:19:43.300 --> 00:19:46.320
you guys are doing all this
awesome evangelism, helping

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:49.360
customers use the platform.

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:51.300
But in the end, you
don't have customers

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:53.670
you are accountable
for, whereas we are.

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:55.560
We actually have a
set of customers who

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:57.960
we are totally responsible for.

00:19:57.960 --> 00:20:02.749
If they don't do well, we are
measured on how well they do.

00:20:02.749 --> 00:20:03.665
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:20:03.665 --> 00:20:06.252
So from a completely
selfish point of view,

00:20:06.252 --> 00:20:08.460
as a developer advocate,
one of the things that we do

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:11.880
is we talk to people and ask
for feedback on our products.

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:15.000
So I'm now wondering
what kind of feedback

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:16.420
do you get from customers?

00:20:16.420 --> 00:20:19.050
What are the things that people
are like, oh, that's so cool?

00:20:19.050 --> 00:20:20.820
Or maybe what are the
kind of things that people

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:21.810
are kind of surprised about?

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:23.851
JONATHAN CHAM: Yeah, I
can talk a lot about that,

00:20:23.851 --> 00:20:26.374
because, yes, I get all
the greatness and all the--

00:20:26.374 --> 00:20:29.040
I don't know if this is a word--
badness from all our customers.

00:20:29.040 --> 00:20:33.315
But the good things I hear
are, I think number one,

00:20:33.315 --> 00:20:35.440
and this is just more from
a technical perspective,

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:36.360
but openness.

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:40.320
We have TensorFlow, Apache
Beam, Kubernetes, Bigtable,

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:41.940
which is HBase.

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:44.490
We have over 300,000
commits on GitHub.

00:20:44.490 --> 00:20:47.100
These are things that no
other cloud vendor really has.

00:20:47.100 --> 00:20:51.990
And it's not as strategic
for them as it is for Google.

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:52.980
Openness is really big.

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:55.470
And for large companies,
this is very important.

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:57.060
They've actually,
in the past, they've

00:20:57.060 --> 00:21:00.310
had vendors that have locked
them in and they can't get out.

00:21:00.310 --> 00:21:02.760
And so for us to really
be pushing this openness

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:04.590
around all these open
source projects where

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:07.050
we can tell them, look,
Google Cloud Platform

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:08.790
is the best place to run.

00:21:08.790 --> 00:21:10.404
But, hey, if we're
not doing our job,

00:21:10.404 --> 00:21:11.820
if we're not making
you happy, you

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:15.180
can always run any of
these on another cloud.

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:17.430
And that's a really
powerful statement.

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:19.350
Another good thing
that I hear is cost.

00:21:19.350 --> 00:21:23.670
Usually we do come lower,
between 20% to 50% lower,

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:25.050
but it really depends.

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:27.300
And I always like
to say we're not

00:21:27.300 --> 00:21:28.950
the Walmart of cloud vendors.

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:32.340
It's really, our cost
is lower because we

00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:35.620
can scale at such high numbers.

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:38.950
We have like 1 to
2 billion users.

00:21:38.950 --> 00:21:41.260
We already have a lot of
infrastructure in place.

00:21:41.260 --> 00:21:44.287
So our economies of
scale are enormous.

00:21:44.287 --> 00:21:45.120
People love our GUI.

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:46.230
I don't know.

00:21:46.230 --> 00:21:47.250
I'd like to ask you.

00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:49.120
Our GUI is pretty
awesome-- the Cloud Shell.

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:49.380
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, yeah.

00:21:49.380 --> 00:21:51.005
JONATHAN CHAM: There's
nothing like it.

00:21:51.005 --> 00:21:52.714
One button, boom,
you're in, ready to go.

00:21:52.714 --> 00:21:54.796
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
probably one of the things

00:21:54.796 --> 00:21:57.210
that I forget about
how cool they are.

00:21:57.210 --> 00:21:59.134
And when I show it to
people that have never

00:21:59.134 --> 00:22:01.050
used Google Cloud Platform,
when they're like,

00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:02.675
oh, you want to
connect to an instance,

00:22:02.675 --> 00:22:04.130
you just click on SSH.

00:22:04.130 --> 00:22:04.630
That's it.

00:22:04.630 --> 00:22:07.290
And people are like, oh.

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:11.134
I forget that that's not like
the normal thing for people.

00:22:11.134 --> 00:22:12.550
JONATHAN CHAM:
Yeah, it's amazing.

00:22:12.550 --> 00:22:14.040
And I don't know if
you know, but they're

00:22:14.040 --> 00:22:15.910
coming out with a code
editor in there, too.

00:22:15.910 --> 00:22:18.180
So really, they're going
to turn that interface

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:21.240
into your single
interface for everything.

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:22.290
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:24.360
All right, so I'm
just curious, I

00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:26.760
think it's fair to have
the good with the bad.

00:22:26.760 --> 00:22:28.329
What are people
finding frustrating?

00:22:28.329 --> 00:22:30.120
JONATHAN CHAM: This is
sort of an oxymoron,

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:33.090
but customers will say we're
not as feature-rich, which

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:35.280
is a totally true statement.

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:36.062
It's factual.

00:22:36.062 --> 00:22:38.520
There's a lot of features that
maybe a lot of cloud vendors

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:39.919
might have that we don't have.

00:22:39.919 --> 00:22:42.210
We're not going to be able
to match feature for feature

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:46.687
with another cloud vendor that's
been in existence for much

00:22:46.687 --> 00:22:47.520
longer than we have.

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:49.920
So, for example,
Postgres, right?

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:52.410
It's a very popular database.

00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:56.100
We launched it in beta
in March of this year.

00:22:56.100 --> 00:22:58.090
But it's a 20-year-old
technology.

00:22:58.090 --> 00:23:00.090
People have been using
it for a very long time.

00:23:00.090 --> 00:23:01.590
And we just announced
that in March.

00:23:01.590 --> 00:23:04.260
And to be fair, we are launching
products like every day.

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:06.634
I don't know how you guys keep
up with the announcements,

00:23:06.634 --> 00:23:09.000
but I get an announcement
every day about a new product.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:09.720
MARK MANDEL: We have a podcast.

00:23:09.720 --> 00:23:10.510
That's how we keep up.

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:11.426
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:23:11.426 --> 00:23:13.440
JONATHAN CHAM: Yeah, yeah.

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:16.110
But I say that it's an oxymoron
because at the same time

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:18.930
we're releasing these kind
of traditional databases.

00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:21.390
We're also coming out, at
the same time, Spanner,

00:23:21.390 --> 00:23:25.290
global availability, global
consistency, like light years

00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:27.510
ahead of what people
actually need.

00:23:27.510 --> 00:23:31.050
But in hindsight, I
think it is Google's job

00:23:31.050 --> 00:23:33.300
to really kind of meet
customers where they are.

00:23:33.300 --> 00:23:35.730
Spanner's great, but
a lot of customers

00:23:35.730 --> 00:23:37.590
aren't ready to
move to something

00:23:37.590 --> 00:23:38.820
that light years ahead.

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:40.800
Former Hadoop
co-creator, he said

00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:42.900
Google's living a few
years in the future

00:23:42.900 --> 00:23:45.090
and sending us messages.

00:23:45.090 --> 00:23:45.780
It's true.

00:23:45.780 --> 00:23:47.010
We have all this technology.

00:23:47.010 --> 00:23:48.990
We've had to scale like crazy.

00:23:48.990 --> 00:23:50.562
So we built all
these new platforms

00:23:50.562 --> 00:23:51.520
to allow us to do that.

00:23:51.520 --> 00:23:53.160
But you think about
all the enterprises

00:23:53.160 --> 00:23:56.400
that have been using on-prem
technologies for the last 20

00:23:56.400 --> 00:23:58.470
years, they're not there yet.

00:23:58.470 --> 00:24:00.330
So what we're doing
in the meantime is,

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:02.880
yep, we're investing in
all these great, awesome

00:24:02.880 --> 00:24:04.980
technologies that we've
already built internally,

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:06.180
externalizing them.

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:08.820
But at the same time,
enabling our customers

00:24:08.820 --> 00:24:11.430
to slowly move their
traditional workloads,

00:24:11.430 --> 00:24:13.590
like leveraging Postgres
and things like that,

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:15.690
to our Google Cloud.

00:24:15.690 --> 00:24:18.420
So I think that's kind of--
for where the features are,

00:24:18.420 --> 00:24:19.439
we're still catching up.

00:24:19.439 --> 00:24:19.980
I don't know.

00:24:19.980 --> 00:24:21.109
What do you guys think?

00:24:21.109 --> 00:24:23.400
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, I
think that, in general, people

00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:26.610
tend to have the view that
since Google Cloud tends

00:24:26.610 --> 00:24:27.840
to be quite--

00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:32.340
it's a pretty young platform
relatively, at least many

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:34.140
of our features are pretty new.

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:38.050
People tend to feel like,
oh, it's not as mature,

00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:38.780
things like that.

00:24:38.780 --> 00:24:42.810
But at the same time, what I
say to that is, well, yeah.

00:24:42.810 --> 00:24:45.437
For instance, Spanner,
it's a pretty new feature.

00:24:45.437 --> 00:24:46.520
It's a pretty new product.

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:48.360
But we've actually been
using it internally

00:24:48.360 --> 00:24:50.850
to power Google for
quite a long time.

00:24:50.850 --> 00:24:52.530
There's an experience
that is internal

00:24:52.530 --> 00:24:54.774
that has helped
us getting there.

00:24:54.774 --> 00:24:56.190
MARK MANDEL: And
that's definitely

00:24:56.190 --> 00:24:58.580
a pattern I've seen
across numerous products.

00:24:58.580 --> 00:24:58.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:24:58.910 --> 00:25:01.020
JONATHAN CHAM: Yeah, I think
the good news is, though,

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:03.380
if you're building a new app,
there's really no feature

00:25:03.380 --> 00:25:04.310
that we're lacking.

00:25:04.310 --> 00:25:06.620
Now, if you have
this old app that

00:25:06.620 --> 00:25:09.590
uses a lot of these kind
of more older technologies,

00:25:09.590 --> 00:25:11.630
there's probably
things that you'll

00:25:11.630 --> 00:25:12.870
need that we might not have.

00:25:12.870 --> 00:25:14.930
But I haven't talked to
a customer where there's

00:25:14.930 --> 00:25:16.760
like this one feature
that's like, oh, OK,

00:25:16.760 --> 00:25:17.790
this is a deal breaker.

00:25:17.790 --> 00:25:19.122
We cannot use Google Cloud.

00:25:19.122 --> 00:25:20.330
There's always a work around.

00:25:20.330 --> 00:25:22.640
And I think that's
what makes us unique.

00:25:22.640 --> 00:25:24.450
We always have things
that you can do,

00:25:24.450 --> 00:25:27.140
that you can work around
any kind of technical block.

00:25:27.140 --> 00:25:29.660
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Do you
get to see more customers

00:25:29.660 --> 00:25:33.860
that are coming to Google
with apps that already exist,

00:25:33.860 --> 00:25:35.840
and they're migrating
them to the Cloud?

00:25:35.840 --> 00:25:38.270
Or do you see more
people that are building

00:25:38.270 --> 00:25:40.490
new infrastructure,
new applications,

00:25:40.490 --> 00:25:42.560
and they're looking
for the Cloud

00:25:42.560 --> 00:25:44.120
as the place to
build them directly?

00:25:44.120 --> 00:25:46.286
JONATHAN CHAM: I think
there's a little bit of both.

00:25:46.286 --> 00:25:48.380
So I actually have
dealt with pretty much

00:25:48.380 --> 00:25:49.450
three types of customers.

00:25:49.450 --> 00:25:51.890
Ones that are already
using a cloud vendor,

00:25:51.890 --> 00:25:55.962
which is the most difficult. You
really have to convince them.

00:25:55.962 --> 00:25:57.920
There's a lot of value
to move to Google Cloud.

00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:01.160
It's not just cost, but there's
an operational cost to move.

00:26:01.160 --> 00:26:04.560
All their employees are already
using another cloud vendor's

00:26:04.560 --> 00:26:05.060
API.

00:26:05.060 --> 00:26:08.060
So there's that migrating
from another cloud.

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:10.810
Number two is
moving from on-prem.

00:26:10.810 --> 00:26:13.940
A lot of these large
enterprises are using on-prem.

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:16.946
They have their own physical
hardware, own data centers,

00:26:16.946 --> 00:26:18.320
and trying to
figure out, OK, how

00:26:18.320 --> 00:26:21.600
do we map that to what
Google Cloud Platform has.

00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:22.920
That's part of our role.

00:26:22.920 --> 00:26:24.620
And then the third
one, our customers

00:26:24.620 --> 00:26:27.170
who are building new apps
already, they kind of just

00:26:27.170 --> 00:26:28.610
said, hey, look, GCP looks cool.

00:26:28.610 --> 00:26:30.230
We're going to start using GCP.

00:26:30.230 --> 00:26:31.730
Oh, it's actually
working very well.

00:26:31.730 --> 00:26:34.188
We're going to continue making
investments in Google Cloud.

00:26:34.188 --> 00:26:36.800
So I think those are the kind
of three profiles of customers

00:26:36.800 --> 00:26:38.210
that I personally see.

00:26:38.210 --> 00:26:39.320
And there might be more.

00:26:39.320 --> 00:26:40.861
MARK MANDEL: So now
I'm curious, what

00:26:40.861 --> 00:26:43.144
are the reasons that you're
seeing people moving?

00:26:43.144 --> 00:26:44.810
JONATHAN CHAM: Right
now I think there's

00:26:44.810 --> 00:26:49.137
a lot of, unfortunately, a lot
of people want to save-- well,

00:26:49.137 --> 00:26:51.470
not unfortunately, but people
want to save money, right?

00:26:51.470 --> 00:26:55.310
And Google Cloud Platform has
a very, very aggressive cost

00:26:55.310 --> 00:26:55.830
structure.

00:26:55.830 --> 00:26:58.190
So in terms of like
storage and compute,

00:26:58.190 --> 00:26:59.870
we are definitely cheaper.

00:26:59.870 --> 00:27:02.870
And while I don't like
to emphasize like, just

00:27:02.870 --> 00:27:04.250
come to us because
we're cheaper,

00:27:04.250 --> 00:27:07.610
we do have a lot of value-add
products like BigQuery.

00:27:07.610 --> 00:27:08.617
I think that's Dremel.

00:27:08.617 --> 00:27:09.700
Dremel is the white paper?

00:27:09.700 --> 00:27:10.480
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:11.646
JONATHAN CHAM: For BigQuery?

00:27:11.646 --> 00:27:14.450
Yeah, you know, we've
had BigQuery internally

00:27:14.450 --> 00:27:18.170
for many years, and
there's nothing like it.

00:27:18.170 --> 00:27:20.450
I always tell a customer,
it's a three-step process.

00:27:20.450 --> 00:27:23.690
Upload your data, write
your SQL query, run it.

00:27:23.690 --> 00:27:25.500
That's very powerful.

00:27:25.500 --> 00:27:27.650
There is no more setting
up infrastructure.

00:27:27.650 --> 00:27:30.060
There's no more updating
and securing patching.

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:34.980
It's just getting real value
from your data instantaneously.

00:27:34.980 --> 00:27:38.299
And so the trend that I'm
seeing across all customers,

00:27:38.299 --> 00:27:40.340
and I think just across
the industry, in general,

00:27:40.340 --> 00:27:42.530
is just people moving
to manage services.

00:27:42.530 --> 00:27:44.930
This is something
that people realize

00:27:44.930 --> 00:27:48.080
that the operational aspect
of managing the infrastructure

00:27:48.080 --> 00:27:50.330
is not very valuable
to their business,

00:27:50.330 --> 00:27:52.190
and they're trying to
get away from that.

00:27:52.190 --> 00:27:54.650
MARK MANDEL: So we're
almost running out of time.

00:27:54.650 --> 00:27:56.954
But is there anything
that we haven't mentioned,

00:27:56.954 --> 00:27:58.370
or you want to
make sure you plug,

00:27:58.370 --> 00:27:59.960
or you just want our
audience to know about?

00:27:59.960 --> 00:28:02.126
JONATHAN CHAM: Yeah, if you
want to reach out to us,

00:28:02.126 --> 00:28:04.640
I run a weekly online meetup.

00:28:04.640 --> 00:28:07.970
Just go to meetup.com/gcponline.

00:28:07.970 --> 00:28:10.640
Actually, if you just
search GCP online meetup,

00:28:10.640 --> 00:28:14.360
we do this weekly webinar
slash architecture slash

00:28:14.360 --> 00:28:16.610
just banter back and forth,
almost like, obviously

00:28:16.610 --> 00:28:18.740
not as cool as a
GCP podcast, but we

00:28:18.740 --> 00:28:21.044
do some kind of a
webinar and demos

00:28:21.044 --> 00:28:23.460
on a lot of these different
products that we talked about.

00:28:23.460 --> 00:28:26.450
So join and subscribe
to us, and we're

00:28:26.450 --> 00:28:27.770
happy to interact with you.

00:28:27.770 --> 00:28:28.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:28:28.190 --> 00:28:29.090
I'll definitely check it out.

00:28:29.090 --> 00:28:29.510
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

